<p>The <strong>Cubii JR1</strong> is the model that started Cubii's budget line: a compact seated elliptical you slide under your desk and pedal while you work. It is manual, quiet and simple, with a resistance dial and a small display. There is no app, no cord and very little to learn.</p>
<p>Since the JR2 arrived, the real question is whether the older JR1 is still worth buying. For a lot of people it is. If you want basic, steady leg movement and you care more about price than refinements, the JR1 does the job. Below we compare the Cubii JR1 vs JR2 and the Cubii Move, and spell out who should buy which, based on specs and owner reviews.</p>

<h2>What Is the Cubii JR1?</h2>
<p>The JR1 is a seated elliptical trainer. Two pedals move in an oval path driven by a resistance flywheel. You turn a dial to make pedaling easier or harder, and the display shows basic stats such as strides, time and estimated calories. It is designed to stay seated: you use it from your normal office chair or a sofa.</p>
<p>The appeal is the same as the rest of the Cubii range. The elliptical motion is easier on the knees than a pedal bike, it is quiet enough for calls, and it is small enough to tuck away. The JR1 simply does this at a lower price than newer models, with fewer extras. You can [[amazon:cubii-jr1|check the current Cubii JR1 listing]] to see how wide the price gap is today.</p>

<h2>Cubii JR1 vs JR2: What Actually Changed</h2>
<p>The JR2 is best understood as a refined JR1, not a different kind of machine. Both are manual, both are seated, both use a resistance dial and a basic display, and neither has Bluetooth (that is the JR2+).</p>
