<p>The honest summary: the pedaling itself is similar. You pay more for the JR2 for polish, a newer design and the confidence of buying the current model. If those things matter to you, the JR2 is worth the extra. If they do not, the JR1 gets you most of the benefit for less.</p>
[[compare:cubii-jr1,cubii-jr2,cubii-move,cubii-jr2-plus]]

<h2>Who Should Still Buy the Cubii JR1?</h2>
<h3>Buyers on a firm budget</h3>
<p>If the JR2 is a stretch, the JR1 gives you a proven, quiet Cubii at a lower price. That beats buying an unknown budget brand to save the same money. If you want to compare it against other low-cost machines, see our <a href="/manual-under-desk-elliptical/">manual under desk elliptical</a> guide.</p>
<h3>People who want light, steady movement</h3>
<p>The JR1 is built for easy, continuous pedaling while you type, read or watch TV. If that is your goal, you do not need more resistance range or extra features.</p>
<h3>Seniors and beginners</h3>
<p>A simple dial, no app and a smooth low-impact motion make the JR1 easy for anyone who is not interested in gadgets. Older buyers often prefer exactly that. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide covers the motorized alternatives if pedaling under your own power is difficult.</p>
<h3>A second machine</h3>
<p>If you already have a Cubii at the office and want one for home, the JR1 is a cheap way to have a machine in both places.</p>

<h2>Who Should Skip the JR1</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>You want app tracking.</strong> Get the [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]], which syncs over Bluetooth. Our <a href="/cubii-pro-hd-review/">Cubii Pro review</a> explains the connected models.</li>
<li><strong>You want a harder workout.</strong> No small Cubii is a high-resistance machine. Look at our list of the best <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk ellipticals with resistance</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You will carry it around a lot.</strong> The lighter Cubii Move is easier to take from room to room.</li>
<li><strong>Price difference is small on the day you buy.</strong> If a sale brings the JR2 close to the JR1, take the newer model.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cubii Move vs JR1</h2>
<p>This is a common comparison because both sit at the lower end of the Cubii range. The [[amazon:cubii-move|Cubii Move]] is designed around portability: it is lighter and meant to be picked up with one hand. The JR1 is the original compact design and stays more planted under a desk.</p>
<p>If your machine will live under one desk, the JR1 is the more stable choice. If you want to use it at your desk during the day and in front of the TV at night, the Move is easier to live with. Either way, a mat helps both stay put on hard floors. Our full <a href="/cubii-move-review/">Cubii Move review</a> includes a Move vs GO vs JR2 breakdown.</p>

<h2>Common JR1 Complaints (and Fixes)</h2>
<p>Owner reviews of the JR1 are broadly positive, but a few complaints come up often enough to plan for. Most are shared with every small seated elliptical rather than specific to this model.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It creeps forward.</strong> Each push nudges the base away from you on smooth floors. A grippy mat, or bracing the machine against a desk leg, solves it.</li>
<li><strong>Knees touch the desk.</strong> This depends on your height, chair and desk, not just the machine. Sitting slightly further back usually gives you the room you need.</li>
<li><strong>Not enough challenge.</strong> Fit users find even the top setting easy. The JR1 is meant for all-day movement, so treat it as a complement to exercise, not a substitute.</li>
<li><strong>Older design.</strong> Some buyers notice it looks and feels less modern than the JR2. That has no effect on how well it moves your legs.</li>
</ul>
<p>If these sound like deal-breakers, read the full list of <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-reviews/">under desk elliptical reviews</a> to see how other brands handle them.</p>

<h2>Where the JR1 Works Best</h2>
<p>Because it is compact and cordless, the JR1 fits in more places than you might expect. At a home office desk it handles the main job. In front of a sofa, it turns TV time into light movement. Some people keep one under a dining table for evening reading or video calls with family.</p>
<p>It is less suited to tall bar-height counters, where your feet may not reach comfortably, and to deep-pile rugs, where the base can rock. A firm, flat surface with a mat gives the smoothest ride.</p>

