<h2>What to Expect Day to Day</h2>
<p>The JR1 works best as background movement. Most owners pedal at a low setting while working and nudge the resistance up for calls or reading. Short, frequent blocks are easier to keep up than one long session, and they break up long stretches of sitting.</p>
<p>In calorie terms, light seated pedaling is roughly 2.0 METs in the Compendium of Physical Activities, compared with about 1.3 for sitting. That means a modest extra burn per hour, which adds up if you pedal through a good part of your day. Here is a quick estimate for your own weight and minutes:</p>
[[tool:calories]]
<p>Wrist trackers often miss seated pedaling, so do not be surprised if your step count barely moves. Our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-steps-calculator/">steps converter</a> turns your minutes into an effort-matched step estimate.</p>

<h2>Cubii JR1 Setup Tips</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check clearance.</strong> Your knees rise at the top of each stride. Use the <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a> before buying.</li>
<li><strong>Stop the slide.</strong> Put the JR1 on a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]], especially on hardwood or tile.</li>
<li><strong>Lock the chair.</strong> If your chair rolls, [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] stop it from drifting back with every push.</li>
<li><strong>Start easy.</strong> Begin with fifteen to twenty minutes at a time at low resistance, then build up as it becomes a habit.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the battery fresh.</strong> If the display goes blank, a new battery is usually the fix.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if your feet slip on the pedals in socks, wear shoes. You get a more consistent push and less strain on your arches.</p></div>

<h2>Buying a Used or Older Cubii JR1</h2>
<p>Because the JR1 has been around for a while, you will see used units for sale. A secondhand Cubii can be a good deal, but check a few things first. Pedal it at every resistance level and listen for clicking or grinding, which usually points to worn parts. Make sure the display turns on and counts strides. And look at the base for cracks, since a damaged housing can rattle under load.</p>
<p>Buying new gives you a return window and a warranty, which is worth a lot on a machine you will use daily. If the price gap between a used JR1 and a new one is small, buy new.</p>

<h2>Verdict: Is the Cubii JR1 Still Worth It?</h2>
<p>Yes, for the right buyer. The JR1 is a quiet, simple, proven seated elliptical that costs less than newer Cubii models. If you want light daily movement and do not care about the latest design or an app, it is an easy recommendation.</p>
<p>If the price difference is small, or you want the most refined ride, choose the [[amazon:cubii-jr2|Cubii JR2]]. To see whether a Cubii is worth using every day at all, read our <a href="/cubii-under-desk-elliptical-is-it-really-worth-using-every-day/">daily Cubii use guide</a>, or let the <a href="/tools/which-under-desk-elliptical-quiz/">60-second quiz</a> pick for you.</p>
